It contains no third-party source code. It depends on the packages below, which are installed separately
(from PyPI) and keep their own licenses. Licenses are those of the versions current at the time of writing;
check each package for the version you install.

Required
  numpy                BSD-3-Clause (bundled components: 0BSD, MIT, Zlib, CC0-1.0)
  pillow               MIT-CMU

Extra [mujoco]
  mujoco               Apache-2.0

Extra [sb3]
  stable-baselines3    MIT
  torch                BSD-3-Clause (bundled components: Apache-2.0, Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception,
                       BSD-2-Clause, BSL-1.0, MIT)
  onnx                 Apache-2.0
  onnxruntime          MIT

Extra [mjlab]
  mjlab                Apache-2.0
  rsl-rl-lib           BSD-3-Clause
  mujoco-warp          Apache-2.0
  warp-lang            Apache-2.0
  torch                as above

mjlab also pulls in viewer, logging and media tools (viser, wandb, tensorboard, trimesh, mediapy,
imageio-ffmpeg, among others). imageio-ffmpeg ships an ffmpeg binary under ffmpeg's own license; an mjlab
block usually installs all of these from its robot package's lock file, so check them there.

The [sb3] dependencies include gymnasium (MIT), absl-py (Apache-2.0), protobuf (BSD-3-Clause), sympy (BSD-3-Clause),
networkx (BSD-3-Clause), jinja2 (BSD-3-Clause) and cloudpickle (BSD-3-Clause). None is GPL-licensed.

On Linux, the torch wheels on PyPI also install NVIDIA CUDA libraries (nvidia-* packages, under NVIDIA's
proprietary license) and triton (MIT). The CPU-only torch wheels from https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu don't;
see README.md, Install.
